      "chapterNumber": 6,
      "headline": "The Narrative Fallacy",
      "keyInsight": "The human brain hates raw chaos. We cannot stomach the fact that luck, timing, and random noise rule most outcomes.\n\nSo we invent clean fairy tales after the dust settles. When a startup wins, journalists write glowing profiles about a visionary founder waking up at four in the morning.\n\nWhen the company goes bankrupt, those same writers claim the founder was reckless and arrogant. Both stories are neat lies tied with pretty ribbons.\n\nRetrospective storylines make history look orderly, predictable, and manageable. But reality is messy, uneven, and riddled with freak events.\n\nIf you mistake a tidy post-game story for an operating playbook, you are navigating real minefields with a cartoon map.",
