      "headline": "Giacomo Casanova's Unfailing Luck: The Problem of Silent Evidence",
      "keyInsight": "We worship winners because dead men tell no tales. Biographers study the daring merchant who risked everything and became an empire builder.\n\nBusiness schools invite reckless CEOs to lecture students on courage. What nobody mentions is the cemetery full of equally bold adventurers who took the identical risk and vanished beneath the waves.\n\nHistory preserves only the survivors and ignores the drowned corpses. This optical illusion makes reckless gambling look like genius strategy.\n\nWhen you evaluate an aggressive move, never judge it solely by the champion standing on the podium. You must always account for the silent graveyard behind him.",
      "content": "An ancient philosopher visited a temple displaying tablets of sailors who prayed during violent storms and survived shipwreck. The priests asked if this was proof that the gods answered prayers. The philosopher replied with a devastating question: where are the portraits of those who prayed and still drowned?\n\nThose men are at the bottom of the sea, and drowned men do not buy commemorative plaques.\n\nGiacomo Casanova famously lived on pure bravado. He escaped prisons, dodged duels, and cheated ruin through sheer audacity. Casanova wrote memoirs detailing his miraculous escapes and concluded he was blessed with destiny.\n\nBut for every Casanova who survived high-stakes gambles, a thousand copycats ended up bankrupt or in unmarked graves. The lucky survivor writes the memoir; the casualties leave no trace.\n\nExecutive boardrooms repeat this error constantly. Leaders copy the aggressive habits of iconic billionaires while ignoring the cemetery of bankrupt competitors who did the same thing. Survivorship bias tricks you into mistaking dumb luck for repeatable skill.\n\nMaster strategists never study just the trophies. They actively dig up the graveyard to measure the true odds of survival.",
